  So the spec carries my own self-review only. That pass found and fixed two real problems, which is worth knowing since
  it's the only review the document has had:

  - [38;5;153mIdentity.clear()[39m was ambiguous about whether logging out discards the device id. Discarding it would re-mint on
    every logout and destroy the correlation the id exists to provide, so it's now split: [38;5;153mclear()[39m drops the user id and
    log, [38;5;153mforgetDevice()[39m drops everything.
  - A test was worded as "login never receives the password," which is false — it's a parameter. Reworded to the
    property that matters: no recorded entry contains the password under any field name.

  [1mYour review[0m

  Spec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-30-login-identity-tracking-design.md[39m (not committed; I added a
  [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m covering [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers[39m so it can't be committed by accident). Please review it and let me know if you
  want any changes before I write the implementation plan.

  Two things in it are worth your attention specifically. The [1massumption[0m in the cutover section — that your future
  backend will accept a client-supplied device id and return a stable user id — is unverified, and it's written as an
  assumption rather than a fact for that reason; it's the one thing that could make part of this design wrong. And the
  [1msecurity section[0m states that the login log is not an audit trail and can be freely edited by the user. If anyone
  downstream is expecting this to answer "who logged in" in a way that holds up under scrutiny, that expectation needs
  correcting now rather than after the backend lands.

  Once you approve the spec, the next step is the writing-plans skill. No code gets written before then.

